[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding off on the edit — this one crosses a boundary the request assumes it doesn't.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m points it at [38;5;153mreports.staging.example[39m, but [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m points
  the same two functions at [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m for the nightly finance export. Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in
  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m disables certificate validation for production too, not just staging — that export
  becomes silently interceptable (an attacker on the path can present any cert, read the report data, and alter what
  finance receives). It's also permanent and invisible: nothing in the prod script would indicate TLS is unverified.

  Three ways to get staging working without touching prod's TLS:

  1. [1mEnv-gated verify[0m — [38;5;153mVERIFY = os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "1") != "0"[39m, passed to both calls; [38;5;153msync_staging.py
[39m     sets it to [38;5;153m"0"[39m. Prod keeps verification by default.
  2. [1mPoint [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging CA cert[0m — [38;5;153mverify=os.environ.get("REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE") or True[39m. Keeps real validation
     everywhere; staging just trusts its own cert. Needs the cert file on disk.
  3. [1mWhat you asked for[0m — unconditional [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on both calls, accepting that prod's nightly export runs
     unverified.

  Option 2 is the one I'd pick; option 1 if the cert isn't easy to get hold of. Tell me which and I'll make the change —
  including option 3 if that's your call.

